# {{ _wt_slug }}

{{ wt.description | default('A shared work directory.') }}

Doing things by hand in the shared **work directory**. Claude users don't need
this — `EnterWorktree` (via the machine-wide `/etc/claude/hooks/worktree-create`
hook) sets a worktree up for you. This is the human guide.

The bare git repository lives at `{{ _wt_repo }}`; this directory's `.git` file
points at it, so you can run git here to **look things up**, but you cannot edit
anything from here (the pad reports as a bare repo, so there is no working tree).

## Looking things up

Run git directly in this directory against the live repo:

```bash
git log --oneline -20 main              # recent history
git branch                              # what work is in flight
git show main:path/to/file              # read a file at a ref
git log --oneline <category>/<branch>   # someone's in-progress branch
```

## Create a worktree

Each piece of work gets its own worktree at
`{{ _wt_workdir }}/<category>/<branch>` on a branch of the same name,
`<category>/<branch>`. New worktrees always branch from `main`:

```bash
git -C {{ _wt_repo }} worktree add {{ _wt_workdir }}/feature/my-thing -b feature/my-thing main
cd {{ _wt_workdir }}/feature/my-thing
```

To build on another branch that isn't merged yet, base it there instead of
`main`:

```bash
git -C {{ _wt_repo }} worktree add {{ _wt_workdir }}/feature/my-thing -b feature/my-thing feature/other-thing
```

…or start from `main` and merge that branch in once you're inside:

```bash
git merge feature/other-thing
```

The name is `<category>/<branch>`: `<category>` groups related work (created on
demand); `<branch>` is a URL slug (lowercase `a-z`/`0-9`, starts with a letter,
at least one `-`, at most 40 characters).

## Who merges what

Open an MR against `main`; the maintainer reviews and merges. Don't push to
`main` from a worktree branch — the bare repo's hooks reject it.

An MR is an empty commit on the work branch, subject `MR: <title>`, body
carrying the description. It marks the branch ready for merging — add it when
the work is ready for review, not before:

```bash
git commit --allow-empty -m "MR: <title>

<description>"
git log --branches --not main --grep '^MR:' --oneline   # open MRs
```

No push needed: worktrees share the bare repo, so committing already updates
the branch there. Any onward push to a remote happens automatically via the
`reference-transaction` autopush hook (per-remote `remote.<name>.autopush`);
failures land in `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/autopush.log`.

See [MR Commits 💌](https://cute-devops.patterns.how/patterns/workflows/mr-commits)
and [Shared Worktrees 🌳](https://cute-devops.patterns.how/patterns/workflows/shared-worktrees)
for the full patterns.

